<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jarnac Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1847</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Anglo-French Convention of 1847</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jarnac Convention was an agreement signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 19 June 1847 in which both powers recognised the independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> of the Society group, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a>, Raiatea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bora-bora">Bora Bora</a>, and agreed not to annex them. It followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-tahitian-war">Franco-Tahitian War</a>, in which France had established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over Tahiti. The convention was abrogated in 1887, and France annexed the islands in 1888.</p></div>
